# 6. THE 180-DAY TRAINING ROADMAP

Create a personalized, editable 26-week curriculum covering 180 days.

Do not blindly assume every beginner progresses at the same rate. Treat the following phases as a starting framework and adapt them using actual performance.

PHASE 1 — FOUNDATIONS (Days 1–14)

Topics:
- What competitive programming is.
- Setting up a C++ compiler and editor.
- Input/output, variables, data types.
- Conditions, loops, functions.
- Integer arithmetic and basic math.
- Arrays and strings.
- Basic implementation.
- Introductory time complexity.
- Reading problem statements.
- Edge cases and debugging.

Outcome:
The user can write small programs independently and understand the input, output, and constraints of a simple problem.

PHASE 2 — BASIC PROBLEM SOLVING (Days 15–35)

Topics:
- Implementation.
- Basic mathematics.
- Frequency counting.
- Sorting.
- Prefix sums.
- Two pointers.
- Basic greedy reasoning.
- Strings and arrays.
- Brute force and optimization.
- Complexity analysis.
- Basic constructive thinking.

Outcome:
The user develops a reliable process for solving straightforward problems without immediately looking at editorials.

PHASE 3 — CORE ALGORITHMIC SKILLS (Days 36–70)

Topics:
- Binary search.
- Greedy algorithms in greater depth.
- Sets, maps, and frequency structures.
- Bit manipulation.
- Elementary number theory.
- GCD and LCM.
- Prime numbers and sieve.
- Recursion.
- Basic dynamic programming.
- Basic graph representations.
- BFS and DFS fundamentals.

Outcome:
The user can recognize common patterns and explain why a proposed solution works.

PHASE 4 — INTERMEDIATE PROBLEM SOLVING (Days 71–105)

Topics:
- Dynamic programming patterns.
- Greedy proof techniques.
- Binary search on answer.
- Graph traversal applications.
- Shortest paths fundamentals.
- Disjoint set union.
- Tree basics.
- More advanced prefix sums.
- Sliding windows.
- Combinatorics.
- Modular arithmetic.
- Problem decomposition.
- Constructive algorithms.

Outcome:
The user can independently approach a meaningful range of intermediate problems.

PHASE 5 — CONTEST READINESS (Days 106–140)

Topics:
- Mixed-topic problems.
- Timed solving.
- Virtual contests.
- Upsolving.
- Implementation accuracy.
- Choosing problem order.
- Time management.
- Debugging under pressure.
- Identifying hidden edge cases.
- Reviewing failed submissions.
- Recognizing familiar patterns in unfamiliar problems.

Outcome:
The user can participate in contests with a deliberate solving strategy and a structured review process.

PHASE 6 — RATING-ORIENTED TRAINING (Days 141–165)

Topics:
- Adaptive problem sets based on actual performance.
- Medium-difficulty problems from weak topics.
- Mixed-topic problem solving.
- More demanding greedy and DP problems.
- Constructive algorithms.
- Binary search and graph applications.
- Regular rated contests when appropriate.
- Virtual contests when live contests are unavailable.
- Detailed upsolving of unsolved problems.

Outcome:
Improve consistency, independent problem-solving ability, and contest performance.

PHASE 7 — FINAL ASSESSMENT AND CONTINUED GROWTH (Days 166–180)

Tasks:
- Review all weak topics.
- Complete targeted problem sets.
- Run timed virtual contests.
- Analyze recent rating changes if available.
- Compare baseline and current performance.
- Build a personal mistake-prevention checklist.
- Assess readiness for the target rating.
- Generate a continuation roadmap if the target is not yet reached.

Important:
A 1600 rating is a goal, not a guaranteed outcome. Do not manufacture progress or declare success solely because 180 days have passed.

For every topic, provide:
- Learning objectives.
- A beginner-friendly explanation.
- Prerequisite concepts.
- Worked examples.
- Practice problems.
- Difficulty progression.
- Common mistakes.
- A mastery checkpoint.
- Revision scheduling.
- Links to reliable learning resources when available.

The user should be able to open any day, inspect the lesson, and resume exactly where they stopped.

